{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "את הנשר", :translit "et ha-nesher", :gloss "the eagle"} {:he "נשר", :translit "nesher", :gloss "eagle"} {:he "נושר", :translit "noser", :gloss "falls off / is shed"} {:he "נוצתו", :translit "nitzato", :gloss "its feather"} {:he "תתחדש כנשר נעוריכי", :translit "tit-chadesh ke-nesher ne'urayich", :gloss "renewed like the eagle's youth"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Wordplay: נשר (eagle) linked to נושר (falls/sheds), explaining that the eagle renews itself by shedding feathers."}], :frame {:text "The eagle is called king among birds, paralleling how the lion is king among beasts. The verse 'renewed like the eagle's youth' (Psalms 103:5) is read as describing the eagle's molting and renewal, shedding old feathers to regain youthful vigor. This same imagery underlies the blessing for a renewed month, where similar language of renewal appears in the liturgy (Shabbat and Rosh Chodesh liturgy).", :sources ["Psalms 103:5"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Wordplay: נשר (eagle) linked to נושר (falls/sheds), explaining that the eagle renews itself by shedding feathers.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :leviticus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-13T00:33:20.847341443Z", :model "glm-5.2"}